1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:30,200
HOST: High in the Andes sits a mountain the Spanish called Cerro Rico, the Rich Hill. In 1545 they struck silver there, and the city below it swelled to two hundred thousand people, as large as London.  The workers called it something else: the mountain that eats men. This is the story of how one mountain wired the first global economy. How did one mountain rewire the world's economy, and what did it cost?

2
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:45,200
HOST: By the end: One: how the mita draft fed workers to Potosí's mines. Two: how American silver caused Europe's price revolution. Three: silver's path from Potosí to the Manila galleons and the first global economy.

3
00:00:45,200 --> 00:01:11,800
HOST: The story goes that in 1545, an Andean herder named Diego Huallpa was chasing lost llamas on Cerro Rico. He built a fire against the cold, and the ground beneath it melted into shining liquid. Silver.  Within years, Potosí was producing most of the world's silver. But Spain had a problem. The mountain was rich, and there was almost no one to dig it.

4
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:34,400
HOST: Spain's answer was the mita. The Inca had used a labor draft called the mit'a for roads and terraces, taking turns from each village. In 1573, the viceroy Francisco de Toledo repurposed it for the mines.  Here is the order, read as it was written. Then listen to a man who lived under it.

5
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:49,000
DECREE: Each village shall send one man in seven to labor in the mines of Potosí for one year. They shall receive a wage. They shall return to their homes when the term is done.

6
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:07,599
MITAYO: They took us from our villages to the cold mountain. We climbed down on rope ladders, candles tied to our thumbs. We crushed ore with our hands and breathed the mercury smoke.  The wage could not buy back our health. Many never came home.

7
00:02:07,599 --> 00:02:31,799
HOST: The silver came out through poison. Miners mixed crushed ore with mercury on open patios, treading it barefoot. The mercury grabbed the silver, and heat burned the mercury away as vapor. It worked on low-grade ore the old smelters could not touch, which is why Potosí kept producing for two centuries. It also poisoned everyone who touched it.

8
00:02:31,799 --> 00:02:55,599
HOST: All that silver crossed the Atlantic to Spain, and then it kept going. More money chasing the same goods meant prices climbed across Europe for a hundred years. By 1600, prices were three to four times higher than in 1500.  Historians call it the price revolution, and it was the first great inflation of the modern world.

9
00:02:55,599 --> 00:03:17,000
HOST: Spain became the richest country in Europe. So Spain won?  Not so fast. Spain spent the silver on wars and imported goods instead of building industry. By the end of the 1500s, the richest empire on earth had gone bankrupt four times. The silver enriched Spain's rivals more than Spain itself.

10
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:48,400
HOST: Follow the silver, in two streams. Potosí's silver sailed east: it crossed the Atlantic to Spain. And from 1565, Mexican silver sailed west: the Manila galleons carried it west across the Pacific, from Acapulco to Manila, trading it for Chinese silk and porcelain. China hungered for silver to run its tax system, and as much as a third of all American silver ended up in Asia. One mountain in the Andes, wired to the whole world.

11
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:58,400
